<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Experimental Details</h2>

<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Details of Datasets with Distribution Shifts</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The 10 public datasets we adopt from TableShift include Voting<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <a href="https://electionstudies.org/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://electionstudies.org/</a></span></span></span>, Unemployment<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <a href="https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/acs" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://www.census.gov/programs-surveys/acs</a></span></span></span>, Sepsis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reyna et al., 2020</a>)</cite>, Public Health Insurance<a href="#footnote5" title="Footnote 5 ‣ A.1 Details of Datasets with Distribution Shifts ‣ Appendix A Experimental Details ‣ LimiX: Unleashing Structured-Data Modeling Capability for Generalist Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, Income<a href="#footnote5" title="Footnote 5 ‣ A.1 Details of Datasets with Distribution Shifts ‣ Appendix A Experimental Details ‣ LimiX: Unleashing Structured-Data Modeling Capability for Generalist Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, Hospital Readmission <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Strack et al., 2014</a>)</cite><span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">6</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <a href="https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/Diabetes+130-US+hospitals+for+years+1999-2008" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://archive.ics.uci.edu/ml/datasets/Diabetes+130-US+hospitals+for+years+1999-2008</a></span></span></span>, Food Stamps<a href="#footnote5" title="Footnote 5 ‣ A.1 Details of Datasets with Distribution Shifts ‣ Appendix A Experimental Details ‣ LimiX: Unleashing Structured-Data Modeling Capability for Generalist Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, Diabetes<span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">7</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/brfss/index.html" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://www.cdc.gov/brfss/index.html</a></span></span></span>, College Scorecard<span id="footnote8" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">8</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <a href="https://collegescorecard.ed.gov/" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://collegescorecard.ed.gov/</a></span></span></span>, and ASSISTments <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Metz, 2020</a>)</cite><span id="footnote9" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">9</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">9</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">9</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <a href="https://new.assistments.org" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter">https://new.assistments.org</a></span></span></span>.
Detailed information of the benchmark TableShift we adopt in <a href="#S7.SS8" title="7.8 Out-of-Distribution Generalization ‣ 7 Evaluation ‣ LimiX: Unleashing Structured-Data Modeling Capability for Generalist Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7.8</span></a> can be found in <a href="#A1.T27" title="In A.1 Details of Datasets with Distribution Shifts ‣ Appendix A Experimental Details ‣ LimiX: Unleashing Structured-Data Modeling Capability for Generalist Intelligence" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T27" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 27: </span>Statistics of datasets in TableShift. ”#Features” indicates the number of features. ”#Train” indicates the number of training samples. ”#ID_test” indicates the number of ID (In-Distribution) test samples. ”#OOD_test” indicates the number of OOD (Out-of-Distribution) test samples. ”Shift Type” indicates how the datasets are split into multiple domains to create distribution shifts. ”#Domains” indicates the numbers of training and test domains.</figcaption>
